Firstmate fork layer
====================

This directory holds everything the Firstmate fork adds on top of upstream
firstmate (https://github.com/kunchenguid/firstmate). Upstream code stays
byte-identical except for two small hook blocks. This file is plain text with
no extension on purpose: upstream's documentation-audience check requires every
tracked *.md, *.mdx, *.rst, and *.txt file to be registered in an upstream-owned
inventory, and the fork does not edit that inventory.


Ownership boundary
------------------

  Upstream core       everything outside fork/ and .github/workflows/fork-ci.yml,
                      unchanged except the two hook blocks below.
  Hook blocks (2)     HK1 in bin/fm-spawn.sh and HK2 in bin/fm-control.sh.
                      Canonical copies: fork/hooks/*.block. They are the only
                      fork edits to upstream files.
  Fork policy         fork/bin/fm-launch-policy: brief intake guard, consult
                      gate, project pins, machine/model resource capacity,
                      Claude subscription.
  ChatGPT channel     fork/chatgpt/: consultation client, loop, plan
                      validator, audit/plan/result contract templates.
  Discord bridge      fork/discord/: gateway and outbox process, sender,
                      inbound process-event extension, optional reply hook,
                      Pi reply extension.
  PR readiness wake   fork/pr-green/: process-event extension (adapter
                      "pr-green"). It ships the adapter, its registration, the
                      conservative readiness predicate that emits the advisory
                      wake when every required check has reported green at a
                      task's recorded head, and the readiness latch that makes
                      each such head wake once (fork/bin/fm-pr-green-teardown is
                      the fork-owned teardown for that latch state).
  Fork checks/tests   fork/bin/fm-fork-hook-check, fork/bin/fm-fork-sync,
                      fork/tests/.
  Fork CI             .github/workflows/fork-ci.yml.
  Home configuration  config/fork-policies, config/fork-project-pins.json,
                      config/fork-resources.json (gitignored, per home).

ChatGPT consultation channel: fork/chatgpt/ (see fork/chatgpt/README); it
writes the consult gate's relaunch delivery proof and needs no upstream edits.

Discord bridge: fork/discord/ (see fork/discord/README). It reaches Firstmate
through upstream's documented extension points - an external process-event
adapter, the reply mirror, and the fleet ledger - and needs no upstream edits.
On a Pi primary, replies arrive through the fork's own Pi reply extension
instead of the mirror (fork/discord/README, "Pi replies").

PR readiness wake: fork/pr-green/ (see fork/pr-green/README). A fork-owned
process-event extension, registered through the same seam as the Discord
inbound adapter. It ships the adapter, its scope gate, its registration, and the
readiness predicate: an open, non-draft pull request of an eligible task, at the
recorded head, whose every unwaived required check has reported green, produces
an advisory wake, while a pending, failed, missing, unreadable, or otherwise
incomplete input stays silent. The wake only ever tells Firstmate to look, and
bin/fm-pr-merge.sh remains the only merge authority and re-validates every
check at merge time. Its readiness latch keeps one wake per task, pull request,
and live head: a changed head re-arms, and a failed marker write or an
interrupted wake repeats rather than suppressing (fork/pr-green/README, "One
wake per head: the latch").

Plan breakdown (the worker-construction step) is part of the ChatGPT channel,
not the bridge: the bridge (codex-chatgpt-web, fm-chatgpt-bridge-lib.sh,
fm-chatgpt-consult.sh) only carries one consultation turn, while the plan
contract and validator make ChatGPT return a dependency-ordered task list
T1..Tn. By default the loop hands that whole list to ONE plan worker.
fork/chatgpt/fm-chatgpt-breakdown.sh instead runs one worker per plan task in
dependency order under consult-gate launch grants; it is disabled unless the
home has config/chatgpt-breakdown, and stays off until a real multi-task run
has passed (fork/chatgpt/README "Plan breakdown"). Exclusive-area
enforcement is not built. Both live under fork/ and need no upstream edits.


The two hook points
-------------------

  HK1  bin/fm-spawn.sh, directly after upstream's project-capacity admission
       and before the brief checks, launch-brief render, endpoint, worktree, and
       task record. Runs for fresh spawns and for fm-spawn --relaunch. A
       refusal leaves nothing behind, like upstream's own capacity deferral.
  HK2  bin/fm-control.sh, inside do_relaunch() after resolve_relaunch_profile
       and before safe_checkpoint/do_exit. A refused relaunch never stops the
       running agent; upstream stops the old agent before calling
       fm-spawn --relaunch, which is why HK1 alone cannot cover relaunch.

Both blocks call fork/bin/fm-launch-policy only when the home has
config/fork-policies. A home without that file behaves exactly as upstream.
With the file present, any non-zero exit refuses - including a missing or
unexecutable policy script.

Hook contract (environment the blocks pass; see the policy script header):
FM_LP_PHASE, FM_LP_TASK, FM_LP_KIND, FM_LP_PROJECT, FM_LP_HARNESS, FM_LP_MODEL,
FM_LP_EFFORT, FM_LP_RAW_LAUNCH, FM_LP_HOME, FM_LP_STATE, FM_LP_DATA,
FM_LP_CONFIG, and from HK1 only FM_LP_OWNER_PID ($$, the launching fm-spawn).
The blocks read these upstream names: in fm-spawn.sh ID KIND PROJ_ABS HARNESS
MODEL EFFORT RELAUNCH RAW_LAUNCH LAUNCH STATE DATA CONFIG FM_HOME FM_ROOT; in
fm-control.sh TARGET_HARNESS TARGET_MODEL TARGET_EFFORT META KIND STATE DATA
FM_HOME FM_ROOT, fm_meta_get, die. fm-fork-hook-check verifies these hook
inputs and the integration boundary (below).

Upstream helper dependencies of claude-subscription: it sources
bin/fm-worker-account-lib.sh (which sources bin/fm-timeout-lib.sh) and uses
fm_worker_account_config, fm_worker_account_resolve, and fm_run_timed; a
missing library or function refuses the launch (exit 2).

Upstream helper dependencies of the resource policy (the consult gate, pins,
and brief-intake use none). The policy sources bin/fm-wake-lib.sh,
bin/fm-secondmate-registry-lib.sh, and bin/fm-backend.sh, and refuses the
launch if any of them cannot be loaded. It then uses:
  agent state       fm_backend_agent_state, fm_backend_of_meta,
                    fm_backend_target_of_meta, fm_meta_get (bin/fm-backend.sh)
  backend loading   fm_backend_source (bin/fm-backend.sh), which loads
                    bin/backends/<name>.sh
  locking           fm_lock_acquire_wait_max, fm_lock_release (bin/fm-wake-lib.sh)
  root home         fm_firstmate_root_home (bin/fm-wake-lib.sh)
  local homes       fm_local_firstmate_state_dirs (bin/fm-wake-lib.sh), which
                    parses registries with secondmate_registry_parse_line
                    (bin/fm-secondmate-registry-lib.sh)
  Herdr processes   fm_backend_herdr_parse_target,
                    fm_backend_herdr_pane_process_state (bin/backends/herdr.sh)
What a missing or failing helper does - always toward refusal or holding:
  - the locking, root-home, local-home, meta, and agent-state helpers above
    are checked when the policy starts; a missing one refuses the launch;
  - fm_backend_source failing makes upstream's fm_backend_agent_state answer
    "unverified", and makes the Herdr re-check print nothing; both hold;
  - a missing Herdr helper makes the Herdr re-check print nothing, which holds;
  - a failing registry parse makes local-home enumeration fail, which refuses.
fm-fork-hook-check --base does NOT verify these helpers: it covers the hook
blocks, their positions and inputs, and the upstream-file boundary. Their
availability is enforced at run time by the policy and exercised by
fork/tests (resource, reservation, and Herdr suites) after every upstream sync.


Policies and configuration
--------------------------

config/fork-policies - one policy name per line (# comments allowed):
  brief-intake | consult-gate | project-pin | resource | claude-subscription
An empty list, an unknown name, or an unreadable file refuses every launch.
Delete the file to turn fork policy off for a home.

brief-intake - ship and scout briefs must carry three intake markers before
any launch (fresh spawn, relaunch, relaunch preflight), so a brief cannot
silently leave firstmate's hands with one of the three defects the
2026-10-29 post-mortem cost review rounds on: slice scope on 'Delivers:' and
'Defers:' lines, the project's check set named on a 'Checks:' line, and a
'Composition:' line saying how bounded promises compose, required once the
brief carries two or more 'Bound:' lines. The refusal names every missing
element with the exact line to add. Secondmate charters are not task briefs
and are never checked; a missing brief is upstream's own refusal.
fork/bin/fm-brief-intake-check owns the markers, and
fork/brief-intake-note states what firstmate must supply in config and in
each brief.

consult-gate - ship and scout launches need one of:
  data/<id>/chatgpt-loop.json        a prompt for the stage its phase puts in
                                     play (audit_prompt at audit-dispatch or
                                     audit-worker; plan at plan-dispatch,
                                     plan-worker, or complete)
  data/<id>/fork-consult-waiver.json {"origin": "local", "reason": "<one line>"}
                                     any other origin, Discord included, refuses
  data/<id>/fork-consult-delivered.json  {"stage": "audit"|"plan"}
                                     relaunch only, never a fresh spawn
  data/<id>/fork-consult-grant.json  a per-task plan worker's launch grant
                                     (fork/chatgpt/fm-chatgpt-breakdown.sh);
                                     checked first, and when present it alone
                                     decides; a loop whose plan runs task by
                                     task refuses a fresh spawn of its own id

project-pin - config/fork-project-pins.json:
  {"projects": {"<project directory name>": {"harness": "pi", "model": "..."}}}
A pinned project refuses another harness or model, and any raw launch command.

claude-subscription - no file of its own. A Claude launch (harness claude,
including a raw claude command), fresh or relaunch, secondmates included:
  - needs upstream's Claude account pin: config/claude-account, or
    config/secondmate-accounts/<id>/claude-account for a secondmate, holding
    'ordinary' or a config root on ONE line. Upstream resolves and checks that
    pin before either hook and sheds the API credentials Claude ranks above a
    stored login; no pin refuses, because without it nothing sheds them;
  - resolves `claude` on this process's PATH and in the worker's login shell
    ($SHELL -lic 'command -v claude', run from the project directory, bounded
    by FM_WORKER_ACCOUNT_CHECK_SECONDS, default 30s) and refuses unless both
    are the same file after symlinks, because the worker types a bare
    `claude` into its pane shell;
  - runs that file's `claude auth status` with only HOME, PATH, TMPDIR, USER,
    LOGNAME (both derived from `id -un` when unset; the macOS keychain login
    reads as signed out without them) and the pinned root, and allows only
    loggedIn with authMethod claude.ai. Signed out, another method (console,
    apiKey, ...), or an unreadable answer refuses.
  It replaces the earlier core edit that added a `subscription` line to
  config/claude-account; upstream's parser refuses that second line.
  Residual gap: a PATH change the pane shell picks up only inside the new
  worktree (direnv, per-directory shims) is not seen; the probe runs from the
  project directory instead.

resource - config/fork-resources.json:
  {"strata": {"capacity": 1, "match": {"model_prefix": "strata-local/"}}}
Resource names match ^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*$. A launch whose model
matches is refused while <capacity> other tasks on a matching model hold a
place, across every project and every local Firstmate home on this machine
(secondmate homes included); secondmates are not exempt. A raw launch is
judged by its own --model and refused if it names none.

A task holds a place when either:
  - its record's endpoint does not read "dead" or "missing" through upstream's
    recovery-grade fm_backend_agent_state ("alive", "ambiguous", "unreadable",
    "unverified", any other answer, or an unreadable record all hold), or
  - it is on Herdr, reads "dead", and its pane is not proven shell-only (below), or
  - it has a valid reservation.

Herdr re-check. Upstream reads a Herdr pane with no registered agent as "dead"
without looking at its processes, so a Pi that is running but has not yet
registered reads like an empty pane. For every Herdr holder that reads "dead",
the policy therefore asks upstream's fm_backend_herdr_pane_process_state
(bin/backends/herdr.sh) for the pane's process view. Only its exact "shell"
verdict - every foreground process is a recognized shell AND no harness
descends from the pane shell in the real process table - frees the place.
"agent" (a harness in the foreground or beneath the shell), "other" (any
non-shell foreground, after upstream's bounded resample), "unreadable"
(process-info failed or was malformed, the shell pid is not in the process
table, or ps is unavailable), and empty or unexpected output (the backend or
helper could not run) all hold it. This blocks conservatively: a pane left with
a stray non-shell process, or whose processes cannot be read, holds the
resource until that task is reconciled. "missing" (pane gone, or the server
positively stopped) still frees without a process check.

Reservations. Admission runs under a machine-wide lock per resource,
<root home>/state/.fork-resource-<name>.lock, taken with upstream's lock
primitive (bounded wait of 10s, then refuse). Under it the policy counts
holders and, when a place is free, atomically writes
<root home>/state/fork-reservations/<name>/<task>.<home hash>.json naming the
launching fm-spawn (FM_LP_OWNER_PID from HK1) by PID and process start time.
The relaunch preflight (HK2) checks without reserving; a relaunch reserves in
HK1. A refused launch removes any reservation it wrote in that run.

Expiry. A reservation stops counting only when its owner is confirmed gone -
ps reports no such process, or the PID now has a different start time (PID
reuse) - AND 120s of startup grace have passed since it was written; it is
then deleted under the lock. An owner that cannot be confirmed either way keeps
the reservation. Age alone never frees a place: a reservation older than 30
minutes is only named in refusals as STALE for reconciliation. Upstream
publishes a task's record before launching its agent, so a running agent always
counts through its record, whatever happens to its reservation.

Recovery needs no manual lock removal: a lock whose owner died is reclaimed by
upstream's stale-owner recovery, and reservations expire as above. A task whose
liveness stays unreadable or ambiguous, or a reservation whose owner cannot be
confirmed, keeps holding until that task is reconciled or cleaned up.
Malformed or unreadable reservations, an unresolvable root home or local home
registry, or an unwritable reservation directory refuse the launch.

Malformed JSON or schema in any of these files refuses the launch.


Manual reservation recovery
---------------------------

Reservations normally need no attention: they expire on their own once their
owner is confirmed gone and the grace has passed. Act only when a refusal keeps
naming the same reservation. Never delete a reservation because it is old - a
STALE label only says that something needs reconciling.

1. Identify it. The refusal names the resource and each holder with a label.
   The reservation file is
     <root home>/state/fork-reservations/<resource>/<task>.<home hash>.json
   and records task, home_state, owner_pid, owner_start, and created. The task
   record, if any, is <home_state>/<task>.meta.

2. Read the label.
   "(launch in progress)"   The launching fm-spawn is still running. Wait. If
                            it is hung, deal with that process through the
                            normal path; never touch the reservation while it
                            lives.
   "(starting, Ns of 120s grace)"  The owner is gone and the agent is not yet
                            visible. Wait out the grace; it expires by itself.
   "(launch owner unconfirmed)"    ps could not confirm the owner. Check:
                              LC_ALL=C ps -o lstart= -p <owner_pid>
                            Exit 1 with no output: owner gone. Output equal to
                            owner_start: still running - wait. Different output:
                            the PID was reused - owner gone. If ps itself fails,
                            fix that first; do not delete anything.
   "STALE reservations need reconciliation (age never frees a place):
    <task>(reserved Ns ago)"
                            Added for any reservation that still holds and is
                            older than 30 minutes; the same task also appears
                            under one of the labels above, which decides what
                            to do. STALE alone is never a reason to remove it.
   A holder named "(ambiguous)", "(unreadable)", "(unverified)", "(no answer)",
   or "(record unreadable)" is a task record, not a reservation: reconcile that
   task through the normal upstream recovery (fm-crew-state.sh, fm-control.sh,
   fm-teardown.sh). Deleting a reservation does not help and is not needed.
   "(herdr pane has no registered agent but its processes read <verdict>)"
                            Also a task record: a Herdr pane with no
                            registration whose processes were not proven
                            shell-only. <verdict> "agent" or "other": a
                            process is still running there - stop or reconcile
                            that task. "unreadable" or "unavailable": the
                            pane's processes could not be read - restore that
                            visibility (Herdr process-info, ps) and recheck
                            with step 3. Deleting a reservation does not help.

3. Confirm the task's agent is gone. The task record must be absent, or its
   endpoint must read dead or missing through upstream's own check:
     FM_HOME=<home> bash -c '. bin/fm-backend.sh; m=<home_state>/<task>.meta;
       fm_backend_agent_state "$(fm_backend_of_meta "$m")" "$(fm_backend_target_of_meta "$m")"'
   and no harness process for that task may be running (ps). On Herdr a
   "dead" pane must also be proven shell-only:
     FM_HOME=<home> bash -c '. bin/fm-backend.sh; fm_backend_source herdr;
       fm_backend_herdr_parse_target "<window>";
       fm_backend_herdr_pane_process_state "$FM_BACKEND_HERDR_SESSION" "$FM_BACKEND_HERDR_PANE"'
   must print exactly "shell"; anything else means a process may still run.

4. Remove only when the owner is confirmed gone (step 2) AND the agent is
   confirmed gone (step 3). Remove exactly that one file, under the resource
   lock so no admission is mid-count:
     FM_HOME=<root home> STATE=<root home>/state bash -c '
       . bin/fm-wake-lib.sh
       lock=<root home>/state/.fork-resource-<resource>.lock
       fm_lock_acquire_wait_max "$lock" 10 || { echo "resource busy; retry"; exit 1; }
       rm -f -- "<reservation file>"
       fm_lock_release "$lock"'

5. A malformed or unreadable reservation refuses every launch on its resource,
   which is the safe state. Fix permissions if that is the cause. Otherwise
   take the task from the file name and apply steps 2-4; if the owner or agent
   cannot be established, leave the file in place and investigate.

6. Never remove the lock path by hand. Upstream reclaims a lock whose owner
   died; a lock that stays busy belongs to a live process, named by the pid
   file in the owner directory its symlink points to.


Known limits
------------

- Startup grace is measured from when the reservation is written, so the
  grace left after fm-spawn exits is 120s minus fm-spawn's own runtime. For pi,
  codex, and claude workers fm-spawn does not wait for the agent: it sends the
  launch, presses Enter, and exits (bin/fm-spawn.sh around the launch send).
- tmux: liveness is read from the pane's processes, so a worker is visible as
  soon as its harness process runs; the launch runs only a local brief-encode
  step before it. The residual window is a launch line that stalls for most of
  the grace before the harness starts.
- Herdr: upstream reads a pane with nothing registered as dead without
  checking its processes (characterised by
  fork/tests/fm-fork-resource-herdr.test.sh). The policy closes that gap with
  the Herdr re-check above, so a running but unregistered Pi keeps holding
  after its reservation's grace. What remains: the re-check trusts Herdr's
  `pane process-info` to name the pane's shell pid and foreground, and the
  window before the harness process starts (the brief-encode step) reads
  shell-only, as on tmux. It has not been run against a real Herdr.
- A refusal by upstream after HK1 leaves the reservation in place. HK1 runs
  before the brief checks, launch-brief render, endpoint, and task record, so
  a launch admitted by the policy can still be refused by upstream afterwards
  (an invalid brief, for example). The policy has already exited, so its
  reservation stays; its owner (that fm-spawn) is then gone, and it expires
  once 120s have passed since it was written - longer only if the owner cannot
  be confirmed gone. Retrying the same task is not blocked by it (a task's own
  reservation is replaced); a different task waits out the grace.
- Relaunch failure window. HK2 checks capacity before the old agent is
  stopped but does not reserve. Upstream then stops the old agent, whose place
  frees, and only reserves again when the replacement reaches HK1 inside
  fm-spawn --relaunch. If another launch takes the place in between (seconds),
  HK1 refuses the replacement: capacity is never exceeded, but the task is left
  stopped - upstream keeps its record, worktree, and progress note and reports
  that the replacement did not launch - until a later relaunch succeeds.
- Only this machine's homes are counted; remote homes and models launched by
  hand outside fm-spawn are not.


Testing ownership
-----------------

Fork CI tests the fork's code and its integration with upstream, not upstream:

  fork/tests/run      runs fm-fork-hook-check and every fork/tests/*.test.sh.
                      It never calls bin/fm-test-run.sh, and fork tests live
                      outside tests/, so upstream's selector, lanes, and
                      coverage guard never see them.
  Hook wiring         fm-fork-hook-check verifies both blocks are present once,
                      identical to fork/hooks/*.block, and at their lifecycle
                      positions, that the upstream names they read exist, and
                      that no other upstream bin/ file references the fork;
                      with --base it verifies nothing else upstream-owned
                      differs from upstream. It does not check the policy's
                      upstream helper dependencies (see "The two hook
                      points"); the fork test suites exercise those.
  Behaviour           spawn and relaunch tests drive the REAL upstream scripts
                      through upstream's shared test libraries (tests/lib.sh,
                      tests/fixtures.sh); the relaunch tests also lift four
                      fixture functions from tests/fm-control-relaunch.test.sh
                      and stop with FIXTURE-DRIFT if those disappear.

Upstream tests are never modified for the fork. Routine fork CI does not run
the upstream suite. Run targeted upstream tests only for a named compatibility
risk, and record the reason.


Upstream sync procedure
-----------------------

fork/bin/fm-fork-sync (its header owns exact behaviour) does the
non-destructive steps; fetching, resolving, fast-forwarding, pushing, and any
cleanup stay manual. Commands run from the active checkout
(~/Projects/firstmate-next); C is the upstream commit to import.

  1. Fetch the commit (the only network step besides the push):
       git fetch --no-tags upstream <sha or main>
       C=$(git rev-parse FETCH_HEAD)          # or the exact sha you named
  2. Prepare an isolated integration (backup ref, branch, worktree, merge):
       fork/bin/fm-fork-sync prepare "$C"
     It writes refs/backup/stage1-enforcement-pre-sync-<short>, creates branch
     fork/sync-<short> in ../firstmate-sync-<short>, and merges there. The
     active checkout, its branch, its untracked notes, and the running
     session are not touched. W below is that worktree.
  3. Exit 3 means conflicts, listed by owner. Resolve in W only:
       hook file   take upstream's version, then re-insert the block from
                   fork/hooks/*.block verbatim at the anchor the README names
                   (git -C "$W" checkout --theirs -- <file>, then edit);
       fork-owned  upstream now writes a fork path: stop, decide by hand;
       upstream    the fork edited an upstream file: a boundary violation.
                   Stop; remove the fork edit (take --theirs) and move the
                   behaviour under fork/ before continuing.
     Then git -C "$W" add <files> && git -C "$W" commit --no-edit.
  4. Check (boundary vs C, ShellCheck, fork tests, review listing):
       "$W"/fork/bin/fm-fork-sync check "$C" --root "$W"
     A lost anchor or contract variable change: re-anchor the block from
     fork/hooks/*.block (never edit the block in place) and rerun. Run extra
     upstream tests only for a named risk, and record why.
  5. Review before landing:
       git -C "$W" log --oneline fork/stage1-enforcement..HEAD
       git -C "$W" diff "$C" HEAD -- bin/          # only the two hook blocks
       git -C "$W" diff fork/stage1-enforcement HEAD --stat
  6. Land and publish (needs the captain's go; this updates the code the
     running session executes, as any self-update does):
       git merge --ff-only fork/sync-<short>      # in the active checkout
       fork/bin/fm-fork-hook-check --base "$C"
       git push origin fork/stage1-enforcement
     --ff-only refuses if the branch moved or if the import would overwrite
     an untracked file; resolve that rather than forcing.

Recovery (nothing here discards landed or pushed work):
  - Abandon an unfinished import: git -C "$W" merge --abort, then
    git worktree remove "$W"; branch fork/sync-<short> and the backup ref
    stay until you delete them deliberately.
  - Undo a local fast-forward that was not pushed, in the active checkout:
       git reset --keep refs/backup/stage1-enforcement-pre-sync-<short>
    (--keep refuses rather than lose uncommitted changes to tracked files).
  - After a push, do not rewrite the branch; fix forward with a new commit.
  - prepare refuses when its backup ref, branch, or worktree already exists:
    finish or deliberately remove the earlier attempt first (--worktree
    only changes the worktree path, not the branch or backup ref names).
